sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant Client as MCP Client<br/>(Autonomous AI Agent, CI/CD, ...)
    participant Proxy as MCP Auth Adapter
    participant SSO as Upstream IdP
    participant MCPServer as MCP Server

    Note over Client, MCPServer: 1. Discovery
    Client->>MCPServer: Connects to server
    MCPServer-->>Client: Announces MCP Auth Adapter as Authorization Server
    Client->>Proxy: GET /.well-known/openid-configuration
    Proxy->>SSO: GET /.well-known/openid-configuration
    SSO-->>Proxy: Returns descriptor (grant_types_supported includes client_credentials,<br/>token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported)
    Proxy-->>Client: Returns filtered descriptor (token_endpoint=adapter/token,<br/>grant_types_supported includes client_credentials,<br/>token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported)

    Note over Client, SSO: 2. Token Acquisition (client_secret_post auth method)
    Client->>Proxy: POST /token (grant_type=client_credentials,<br/>client_id, client_secret, scope)
    Proxy->>SSO: POST /token (grant_type=client_credentials,<br/>client_id, client_secret, scope)
    SSO->>SSO: Validates client credentials
    SSO-->>Proxy: Returns Access Token
    Proxy-->>Client: Forwards Access Token

    Note over Client, MCPServer: 3. Secure Resource Access
    Client->>MCPServer: Sends MCP request (e.g. tool call) with Access Token
    MCPServer->>Proxy: GET /.well-known/openid-configuration
    Proxy-->>MCPServer: Returns descriptor containing JWKS (Certificate) URI from Upstream IdP
    MCPServer-->>SSO: Fetches Certificate used to sign Access Token
    MCPServer->>MCPServer: Validates the Access Token signature & claims<br/>Authenticates and authorizes request